The function \fBmb_version\fP returns the \fBMB-System\fP release
version as both a string \fI*version_string\fP (e.g. "5.8.0") and,
parsed from that string, the major, minor, and archive version
numbers and a single combined numeric \fI*version_id\fP (e.g.
version 5.8.2303 becomes 50802303) suitable for numeric
comparisons.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_user_host_date\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		char \fIuser[256]\fP,
 		char \fIhost[256]\fP,
 		char \fIdate[32]\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_user_host_date\fP returns the current user
name \fIuser\fP (from the \fBUSER\fP or \fBLOGNAME\fP environment
variable), host name \fIhost\fP, and current date and time
\fIdate\fP as strings. This is used by many \fBMB-System\fP
programs to record who processed a file, on what machine, and
when, typically in a processing comment record.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_default_defaults\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		int \fI*format\fP,
 		int \fI*pings\fP,
 		int \fI*lonflip\fP,
 		double \fIbounds[4]\fP,
 		int \fI*btime_i\fP,
 		int \fI*etime_i\fP,
 		double \fI*speedmin\fP,
 		double \fI*timegap\fP);

The function \fBmb_default_defaults\fP sets the hardwired
\fBMBIO\fP default control parameter values in the same manner as
\fBmb_defaults\fP below, but without consulting a possible
\fI.mbio_defaults\fP file in the user's home directory.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_defaults\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		int \fI*format\fP,
 		int \fI*pings\fP,
 		int \fI*lonflip\fP,
 		double \fIbounds[4]\fP,
 		int \fI*btime_i\fP,
 		int \fI*etime_i\fP,
 		double \fI*speedmin\fP,
 		double \fI*timegap\fP);

The function \fBmb_defaults\fP provides default values of control
parameters used by some of the \fBMBIO\fP functions. The \fIverbose\fP
value controls the standard error output verbosity of the function.
The other parameters
are set by the function; the meaning of these parameters is discussed
in the listings of the functions
\fBmb_read_init\fP and \fBmb_write_init\fP.
If an .mbio_defaults file exists in the user's home directory,
the lonflip and timegap defaults are read from this file.
Otherwise, the values are set as:
 	\fI*lonflip\fP = 0
 	\fI*timegap\fP = 1
.br
The other values are simply set as:
 	\fI*format\fP = 0
 	\fI*pings\fP = 1
 	\fIbounds[0]\fP = \-360.
 	\fIbounds[1]\fP = 360.
 	\fIbounds[2]\fP = \-90.
 	\fIbounds[3]\fP = 90.
 	\fIbtime_i[0]\fP = 1962;
 	\fIbtime_i[1]\fP = 2;
 	\fIbtime_i[2]\fP = 21;
 	\fIbtime_i[3]\fP = 10;
 	\fIbtime_i[4]\fP = 30;
 	\fIbtime_i[5]\fP = 0;
 	\fIbtime_i[6]\fP = 0;
 	\fIetime_i[0]\fP = 2062;
 	\fIetime_i[1]\fP = 2;
 	\fIetime_i[2]\fP = 21;
 	\fIetime_i[3]\fP = 10;
 	\fIetime_i[4]\fP = 30;
 	\fIetime_i[5]\fP = 0;
 	\fIetime_i[6]\fP = 0;
 	\fI*speedmin\fP = 0.0

A status value is returned to
indicate success or failure.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_lonflip\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		int \fI*lonflip\fP);

The function \fBmb_lonflip\fP returns just the default
\fI*lonflip\fP value described above under \fBmb_defaults\fP,
reading it from a \fI.mbio_defaults\fP file in the user's home
directory if one exists (otherwise 0).

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_fbtversion\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		int \fI*fbtversion\fP);

The function \fBmb_fbtversion\fP returns the version number of the
fast bathymetry (\fI.fbt\fP) file format to be generated by
\fBmb_make_info\fP and related functions, reading it from a
\fI.mbio_defaults\fP file in the user's home directory if one
exists (otherwise 3, the current \fI.fbt\fP format version).

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_uselockfiles\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		bool \fI*uselockfiles\fP);

The function \fBmb_uselockfiles\fP returns true if \fBMBIO\fP
should create lock files to prevent simultaneous editing of swath
data files by multiple programs (e.g. \fBmbedit\fP), reading this
setting from a \fI.mbio_defaults\fP file in the user's home
directory if one exists (otherwise true). On Windows,
\fI*uselockfiles\fP is always returned false because lock file
creation is not supported there.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_fileiobuffer\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		int \fI*fileiobuffer\fP);

The function \fBmb_fileiobuffer\fP returns the size (in some
implementation-defined unit) of the buffer to be used for low
level file input/output by \fBmb_fileio_open\fP, reading this
setting from a \fI.mbio_defaults\fP file in the user's home
directory if one exists (otherwise 0, meaning the system default
buffering is used).

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_mbview_defaults\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		int \fI*primary_colortable\fP,
 		int \fI*primary_colortable_mode\fP,
 		int \fI*primary_shade_mode\fP,
 		int \fI*slope_colortable\fP,
 		int \fI*slope_colortable_mode\fP,
 		int \fI*secondary_colortable\fP,
 		int \fI*secondary_colortable_mode\fP,
 		double \fI*illuminate_magnitude\fP,
 		double \fI*illuminate_elevation\fP,
 		double \fI*illuminate_azimuth\fP,
 		double \fI*slope_magnitude\fP);

The function \fBmb_mbview_defaults\fP returns default color table,
shading mode, and illumination parameter values used by the
\fBmbview\fP 3D visualization widget, reading them from a
\fI.mbio_defaults\fP file in the user's home directory if one
exists.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_env\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		char \fI*psdisplay\fP,
 		char \fI*imgdisplay\fP,
 		char \fI*mbproject\fP);

The function \fBmb_env\fP provides default values of Postscript
and image display programs invoked by some \fBMB-System\fP
programs and macros, and a default value for a working project
name that will be used by future applications. The \fIverbose\fP
value controls the standard error output verbosity of the function.
If an .mbio_defaults file exists in the user's home directory,
the \fI*psdisplay\fP, \fI*imgdisplay\fP, \fI*mbproject\fP
defaults are read from this file.
Otherwise, the values are set as:
 	\fIpsdisplay\fP =  "xpsview" (IRIX OS)
 	                   "pageview" (Solaris OS)
 	                   "gv" (other OS)
 	                   "ghostview" (other OS)
 	\fIimgdisplay\fP = "gimp" (Linux OS)
 	                   "xv" (other than Linux OS)
 	\fImbproject\fP =  "none"

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_format\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		int \fI*format\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

Given the format identifier \fIformat\fP, \fBmb_format\fP checks if the
format is valid.  If the format id corresponds to a value used in
previous (<4.00) versions of \fBMB-System\fP, then the format value
will be aliased to the current corresponding value.

The return values are:
  	\fIformat\fP:		\fBMBIO\fP format id
  	\fIerror\fP:		error value

A status value indicating success or failure is returned; the error value
argument \fIerror\fP passes more detailed information about failures.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_format_register\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		int \fI*format\fP,
 		void \fI*mbio_ptr\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_format_register\fP is called by \fBmb_read_init\fP
and \fBmb_write_init\fP and serves to load format specific
parameters and function parameters into the \fBMBIO\fP
control structure pointed to by \fI*error\fP. The format id
\fI*format\fP is first checked for validity. In some cases,
formerly valid but now obsolete format id values are mapped
to current values.

The input values are:
 	\fI*format\fP:		\fBMBIO\fP format id
 	\fI*mbio_ptr\fP:	pointer to data in specified
 				buffer record

The return values are:
  	\fIerror\fP:		error value

A status value indicating success or failure is returned; the error value
argument \fIerror\fP passes more detailed information about failures.

