 	\fI*format\fP:		\fBMBIO\fP format id
 	\fI*variable_beams\fP:	number of beams can vary [boolean]
 	\fI*traveltime\fP:	travel time data available [boolean]
 	\fI*beam_flagging\fP:	beam flagging supported [boolean]
   	\fIerror\fP:		error value
.br
A status value indicating success or failure is returned; the error value
argument \fIerror\fP passes more detailed information about failures.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_format_source\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		int \fI*format\fP,
 		int \fI*platform_source\fP,
 		int \fI*nav_source\fP,
 		int \fI*sensordepth_source\fP,
 		int \fI*heading_source\fP,
 		int \fI*attitude_source\fP,
 		int \fI*svp_source\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_format_source\fP returns
flags indicating what kinds of data records contain
platform, navigation, sensor depth, heading, attitude, and
sound velocity profile values in the
specified data format. The format id
\fI*format\fP is first checked for validity. In some cases,
formerly valid but now obsolete format id values are mapped
to current values. The input values are:
 	\fI*format\fP:		\fBMBIO\fP format id
.br
The return values are:
 	\fI*format\fP:		\fBMBIO\fP format id
 	\fI*platform_source\fP:	kind of data records containing
 				sensor platform information
 	\fI*nav_source\fP:	kind of data records containing
 				navigation
 	\fI*sensordepth_source\fP:	kind of data records containing
 				sensor (transducer) depth
 	\fI*heading_source\fP:	kind of data records containing
 				heading
 	\fI*attitude_source\fP:	kind of data records containing
 				attitude
 	\fI*svp_source\fP:	kind of data records containing
 				sound velocity profiles
   	\fIerror\fP:		error value
.br
A status value indicating success or failure is returned; the error value
argument \fIerror\fP passes more detailed information about failures.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_format_beamwidth\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		int \fI*format\fP,
 		double \fI*beamwidth_xtrack\fP,
 		double \fI*beamwidth_ltrack\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_format_beamwidth\fP returns
typical, upper bound values for athwartships and alongtrack
beam widths. The format id
\fI*format\fP is first checked for validity. In some cases,
formerly valid but now obsolete format id values are mapped
to current values. The input values are:
 	\fI*format\fP:		\fBMBIO\fP format id
.br
The return values are:
 	\fI*format\fP:		\fBMBIO\fP format id
 	\fI*beamwidth_xtrack\fP:	typical athwartships beam
 					width [degrees]
 	\fI*beamwidth_ltrack\fP:	typical alongtrack beam
 					width [degrees]
   	\fIerror\fP:		error value
.br
A status value indicating success or failure is returned; the error value
argument \fIerror\fP passes more detailed information about failures.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_get_format\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		char \fI*filename\fP,
 		char \fI*fileroot\fP,
 		int \fI*format\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_get_format\fP attempts to determine the
\fBMBIO\fP format id \fI*format\fP of \fIfilename\fP from a
recognized filename suffix convention (e.g. \fI.mbXXX\fP,
\fI.mb1\fP, or \fI.nve\fP), and if successful returns in
\fI*fileroot\fP the part of \fIfilename\fP preceding that suffix.
If no recognized suffix is found, \fI*format\fP is returned as 0.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_get_relative_path\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		char \fI*path\fP,
 		char \fI*pwd\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_get_relative_path\fP rewrites \fI*path\fP in
place, replacing it with the equivalent path relative to the
working directory \fIpwd\fP, if \fIpath\fP lies within (or below)
\fIpwd\fP. This is used, for instance, by \fBmbdatalist\fP so that
datalist files can reference data files with relative rather than
absolute paths.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_get_absolute_path\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		char \fI*path\fP,
 		char \fI*pwd\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_get_absolute_path\fP rewrites \fI*path\fP in
place, replacing a relative path with the equivalent absolute path
obtained by prepending the working directory \fIpwd\fP, if
\fIpath\fP is not already absolute.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_get_shortest_path\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		char \fI*path\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_get_shortest_path\fP rewrites \fI*path\fP in
place with the shortest equivalent path, removing redundant
\fI./\fP and \fI../\fP path components.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_get_basename\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		char \fI*path\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_get_basename\fP rewrites \fI*path\fP in place,
replacing it with just its final path component (the part
following the last "/").

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_cvt_to_nix_path\fP(
 		char \fI*path\fP);

The function \fBmb_cvt_to_nix_path\fP, available only when
\fBMB-System\fP is built for Windows, rewrites \fIpath\fP in place,
converting a Windows-style path (using "\\e" separators and a
drive letter) into the equivalent Unix-style path (using "/"
separators, with any drive letter removed) used internally by
\fBMBIO\fP.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_datalist_open\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI**datalist_ptr\fP,
 		char \fI*path\fP,
 		int \fIlook_processed\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_datalist_open\fP initializes reading
from a datalist tree. The string \fI*path\fP is the
path to the top level datalist file to be opened.
The value \fIlook_processed\fP indicates whether the datalist
parsing should look for or ignore processed data files
(see the \fBmbprocess\fP and \fBmbdatalist\fP manual pages).
The input values are:
 	\fI*path\fP:		datalist file to be opened
 	\fIlook_processed\fP:	processed file behavior
 					0 : unset
 					1 : ignore processed files
 					2 : return processed files
.br
The return values are:
 	\fI**datalist_ptr\fP:	pointer to datalist
 				structure
   	\fIerror\fP:		error value
.br
A status value indicating success or failure is returned; the error value
argument \fIerror\fP passes more detailed information about failures.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_datalist_read\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*datalist_ptr\fP,
 		char \fI*path\fP,
 		char \fI*dpath\fP,
 		int \fI*format\fP,
 		double \fI*weight\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_datalist_read\fP reads
from a datalist tree, attempting to return the path
to the next valid swath data file, the corresponding data
format id, and a gridding weight
(see the \fBmbprocess\fP and \fBmbdatalist\fP manual pages).
If a processed version of the data file exists and processed
files are to be used, \fI*path\fP is returned as the path to the
processed file. Information about the
datalist tree is embedded in a data structure pointed to
by \fI*datalist_ptr\fP. The input values are:
 	\fI*datalist_ptr\fP:	pointer to datalist
 				structure
.br
The return values are:
 	\fI*path\fP:		swath data file (or its processed
 				equivalent, if used)
 	\fI*dpath\fP:		path of the datalist file referencing
 				\fI*path\fP
 	\fI*format\fP:		\fBMBIO\fP format id
 	\fI*weight\fP:		\fBmbgrid\fP gridding weight
   	\fIerror\fP:		error value
.br
A status value indicating success or failure is returned; the error value
argument \fIerror\fP passes more detailed information about failures.
This function is implemented as a call to \fBmb_datalist_read3\fP.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_datalist_readorg\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*datalist_ptr\fP,
 		char \fI*path\fP,
 		int \fI*format\fP,
 		double \fI*weight\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_datalist_readorg\fP reads
from a datalist tree exactly as \fBmb_datalist_read\fP does, except
that the original (unprocessed) file path is always returned in
\fI*path\fP, even when a processed version of the file exists.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_datalist_read2\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*datalist_ptr\fP,
 		int \fI*pstatus\fP,
 		char \fI*path\fP,
 		char \fI*ppath\fP,
 		char \fI*dpath\fP,
 		int \fI*format\fP,
 		double \fI*weight\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_datalist_read2\fP reads
from a datalist tree, returning both the original file path
\fI*path\fP and, separately, the path \fI*ppath\fP of the
processed version of that file (if one exists). The value
\fI*pstatus\fP indicates whether a processed file exists
(\fBMB_PROCESSED_EXIST\fP) and if so whether it should be used
(\fBMB_PROCESSED_USE\fP) or not (\fBMB_PROCESSED_NONE\fP).
\fI*dpath\fP is returned as the path of the datalist file
that references \fI*path\fP.
\fBmb_datalist_read\fP is implemented as a call to
\fBmb_datalist_read3\fP, which is in turn implemented as a call
to \fBmb_datalist_read2\fP with the addition of alternative
navigation file handling.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_datalist_read3\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*datalist_ptr\fP,
 		int \fI*pstatus\fP,
 		char \fI*path\fP,
 		char \fI*ppath\fP,
 		int \fI*astatus\fP,
 		char \fI*apath\fP,
 		char \fI*dpath\fP,
 		int \fI*format\fP,
 		double \fI*weight\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_datalist_read3\fP reads
from a datalist tree exactly as \fBmb_datalist_read2\fP does, and in
addition returns the path \fI*apath\fP to an alternative navigation
file associated with the data file, if one is specified in the
datalist. The value \fI*astatus\fP indicates whether an alternative
navigation file was found (\fBMB_ALTNAV_USE\fP) or not
(\fBMB_ALTNAV_NONE\fP).

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_datalist_recursion\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*datalist_ptr\fP,
 		bool \fIprint\fP,
 		int \fI*recursion\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_datalist_recursion\fP returns the current
recursion depth of the datalist tree rooted at \fI*datalist_ptr\fP
(datalist files may reference other datalist files, and this value
tracks how deeply nested the currently open file is). If
\fIprint\fP is true, a summary of the recursive datalist structure
is printed to standard error.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_datalist_close\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI**datalist_ptr\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_datalist_close\fP closes an
open datalist tree, and deallocates the data structure pointed to
by \fI*datalist_ptr\fP. The input values are:
 	\fI*datalist_ptr\fP:	pointer to datalist
 				structure
.br
The return values are:
   	\fIerror\fP:		error value
.br
A status value indicating success or failure is returned; the error value
argument \fIerror\fP passes more detailed information about failures.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_imagelist_open\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI**imagelist_ptr\fP,
 		char \fI*path\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_imagelist_open\fP initializes reading
from an imagelist tree in the same manner that
\fBmb_datalist_open\fP does for a datalist tree. An imagelist
references still or video camera image files (and, for stereo
cameras, pairs of image files) associated with a survey, along
with per-image navigation and timing metadata used when
merging images with swath data
(see the \fBmbphotomosaic\fP manual page). The string \fI*path\fP
is the path to the top level imagelist file to be opened.
The return values are:
 	\fI**imagelist_ptr\fP:	pointer to imagelist
 				structure
   	\fIerror\fP:		error value
.br
A status value indicating success or failure is returned; the error value
argument \fIerror\fP passes more detailed information about failures.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_imagelist_read\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*imagelist_ptr\fP,
 		int \fI*imagestatus\fP,
 		bool \fI*rectified\fP,
 		char \fI*path0\fP,
 		char \fI*path1\fP,
 		char \fI*dpath\fP,
 		double \fI*time_d0\fP,
 		double \fI*time_d1\fP,
 		double \fI*gain0\fP,
 		double \fI*gain1\fP,
 		double \fI*exposure0\fP,
 		double \fI*exposure1\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_imagelist_read\fP reads
from an imagelist tree, attempting to return the path (or, for a
stereo pair, paths) to the next image file along with its
timestamp(s), camera gain(s), and exposure time(s). The value
\fI*imagestatus\fP indicates the kind of image record obtained
(e.g. single image or stereo pair), and \fI*rectified\fP indicates
whether the image(s) have already been rectified. The return
values are:
 	\fI*path0\fP:		path of the (first) image file
 	\fI*path1\fP:		path of the second image file of a
 				stereo pair, if any
 	\fI*dpath\fP:		path of the imagelist file referencing
 				\fI*path0\fP and \fI*path1\fP
 	\fI*time_d0\fP:		time stamp of first image
 	\fI*time_d1\fP:		time stamp of second image, if any
 	\fI*gain0\fP:		camera gain for first image
 	\fI*gain1\fP:		camera gain for second image, if any
 	\fI*exposure0\fP:	exposure time for first image
 	\fI*exposure1\fP:	exposure time for second image, if any
   	\fIerror\fP:		error value
.br
A status value indicating success or failure is returned; the error value
argument \fIerror\fP passes more detailed information about failures.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_imagelist_recursion\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*imagelist_ptr\fP,
 		bool \fIprint\fP,
 		int \fI*recursion\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_imagelist_recursion\fP returns the current
recursion depth of the imagelist tree rooted at
\fI*imagelist_ptr\fP, in the same manner that
\fBmb_datalist_recursion\fP does for a datalist tree. If
\fIprint\fP is true, a summary of the recursive imagelist
structure is printed to standard error.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_imagelist_close\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI**imagelist_ptr\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_imagelist_close\fP closes an
open imagelist tree, and deallocates the data structure pointed
to by \fI*imagelist_ptr\fP.
A status value indicating success or failure is returned; the error value
argument \fIerror\fP passes more detailed information about failures.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_check_info\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		char \fI*file\fP,
 		int \fIlonflip\fP,
 		double \fIbounds[4]\fP,
 		bool \fI*file_in_bounds\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_check_info\fP checks whether the swath data
file \fIfile\fP has any data within the region specified by
\fIlonflip\fP and \fIbounds\fP, without opening or reading the
data file itself. This is done by reading the \fBmbinfo\fP summary
statistics from the associated \fI.inf\fP file (see the
\fBmbinfo\fP and \fBmbdatalist\fP manual pages), which lists the
geographic bounds of the data. If \fIfile\fP is "stdin" or no
\fI.inf\fP file exists, \fI*file_in_bounds\fP is simply set true.
This function is used by \fBmbdatalist\fP and other programs
to quickly exclude files with no data in a region of interest
without opening every file in a large datalist.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
bool \fBmb_should_make_fbt\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		int \fIformat\fP);

The function \fBmb_should_make_fbt\fP returns true if swath data
files of the given \fBMBIO\fP \fIformat\fP benefit from having an
associated fast bathymetry file (\fI.fbt\fP suffix; see
\fBmb_get_fbt\fP below) generated for them, and false for formats
(e.g. navigation-only or ASCII formats) for which a \fI.fbt\fP
file is not useful.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
bool \fBmb_should_make_fnv\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		int \fIformat\fP);

The function \fBmb_should_make_fnv\fP returns true if swath data
files of the given \fBMBIO\fP \fIformat\fP benefit from having an
associated fast navigation file (\fI.fnv\fP suffix; see
\fBmb_get_fnv\fP below) generated for them, and false otherwise.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_make_info\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		bool \fIforce\fP,
 		char \fI*file\fP,
 		int \fIformat\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_make_info\fP checks the modification times of
the \fI.inf\fP, \fI.fbt\fP, and \fI.fnv\fP ancillary files
associated with the swath data file \fIfile\fP against the
modification time of \fIfile\fP itself, and regenerates any that
are missing or out of date by invoking \fBmbinfo\fP (for the
\fI.inf\fP file) and \fBmbcopy\fP (for the \fI.fbt\fP and
\fI.fnv\fP files) as subprocesses. If \fIforce\fP is true, the
ancillary files are regenerated unconditionally.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_make_info_datalist\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		bool \fIforce\fP,
 		char \fI*read_file\fP,
 		int \fI*format\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_make_info_datalist\fP calls \fBmb_make_info\fP
for every swath data file referenced (directly or recursively) by
the datalist or single swath file named \fIread_file\fP.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_get_fbt\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		char \fI*file\fP,
 		int \fI*format\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_get_fbt\fP checks whether a fast bathymetry
file (\fIfile\fP with a \fI.fbt\fP suffix appended) exists and is
present; if so \fI*file\fP is replaced with the path of the
\fI.fbt\fP file and \fI*format\fP is set to the fast bathymetry
format id (71). If no \fI.fbt\fP file exists, \fI*file\fP and
\fI*format\fP are left unchanged. This allows an application such
as \fBmbgrid\fP to transparently read the much smaller and faster
\fI.fbt\fP file in place of the original data file when one is
available.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_get_fnv\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		char \fI*file\fP,
 		int \fI*format\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_get_fnv\fP behaves exactly as \fBmb_get_fbt\fP
does, except that it looks for a fast navigation file (a
\fI.fnv\fP suffix) and, if found, sets \fI*format\fP to the fast
navigation format id (166).

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_get_ffa\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		char \fI*file\fP,
 		int \fI*format\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_get_ffa\fP behaves exactly as \fBmb_get_fbt\fP
does, except that it looks for a fast amplitude file (a
\fI.ffa\fP suffix) and, if found, sets \fI*format\fP to the fast
bathymetry/amplitude format id (71).

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_get_ffs\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		char \fI*file\fP,
 		int \fI*format\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_get_ffs\fP behaves exactly as \fBmb_get_fbt\fP
does, except that it looks for a fast sidescan file (a
\fI.ffs\fP suffix) and, if found, sets \fI*format\fP to the fast
bathymetry/sidescan format id (71).

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_swathbounds\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		int \fIcheckgood\fP,
 		int \fInbath\fP,
 		int \fInss\fP,
 		char \fI*beamflag\fP,
 		double \fI*bathacrosstrack\fP,
 		double \fI*ss\fP,
 		double \fI*ssacrosstrack\fP,
 		int \fI*ibeamport\fP,
 		int \fI*ibeamcntr\fP,
 		int \fI*ibeamstbd\fP,
 		int \fI*ipixelport\fP,
 		int \fI*ipixelcntr\fP,
 		int \fI*ipixelstbd\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_swathbounds\fP examines the bathymetry and
sidescan acrosstrack distance arrays of a ping (of \fInbath\fP
beams and \fInss\fP pixels) and returns the port-most, center, and
starboard-most beam and pixel indices. If \fIcheckgood\fP is set,
beams and pixels flagged bad are excluded from consideration. This
is used by plotting and processing programs (e.g. \fBmbedit\fP and
\fBmbeditviz\fP) to quickly determine the acrosstrack extent of a
ping's good data.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_info_init\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		struct mb_info_struct \fI*mb_info\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_info_init\fP initializes (zeroes) the fields
of the \fIstruct mb_info_struct\fP pointed to by \fImb_info\fP, the
data structure used by \fBmb_get_info\fP to hold the summary
statistics parsed from an \fI.inf\fP file.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_get_info\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		char \fI*file\fP,
 		struct mb_info_struct \fI*mb_info\fP,
 		int \fIlonflip\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_get_info\fP reads the \fBmbinfo\fP summary
statistics for the swath data file \fIfile\fP from its associated
\fI.inf\fP file (generating that file first with \fBmb_make_info\fP
if necessary) and returns them in the \fIstruct mb_info_struct\fP
pointed to by \fImb_info\fP. The \fIlonflip\fP value controls how
longitude values are wrapped when read from the \fI.inf\fP file.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_get_info_datalist\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		char \fI*read_file\fP,
 		int \fI*format\fP,
 		struct mb_info_struct \fI*mb_info\fP,
 		int \fIlonflip\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_get_info_datalist\fP behaves as
\fBmb_get_info\fP does, but accepts a datalist as well as a single
swath file for \fIread_file\fP; when given a datalist, the summary
statistics returned in \fImb_info\fP are accumulated over every
swath file referenced by the datalist.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_alloc\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*mbio_ptr\fP,
 		void \fI**store_ptr\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_alloc\fP allocates a data structure
for internal storage of swath sonar data
and returns a pointer to this structure in \fI*store_ptr\fP.
The data structure is specific to the data format identified
in the \fBMBIO\fP data structure pointed to by \fI*mbio_ptr\fP.
The input values are:
 	\fI*mbio_ptr\fP:	pointer to \fBMBIO\fP structure
.br
The return values are:
 	\fI**store_ptr\fP:	pointer to storage data
 				structure
   	\fIerror\fP:		error value
.br
A status value indicating success or failure is returned; the error value
argument \fIerror\fP passes more detailed information about failures.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_deall\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*mbio_ptr\fP,
 		void \fI**store_ptr\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_deall\fP deallocates a format specific
swath sonar data structure pointed to by \fI*store_ptr\fP.
The input values are:
 	\fI*mbio_ptr\fP:	pointer to \fBMBIO\fP structure
 	\fI*store_ptr\fP:	pointer to storage data
 				structure
.br
The return values are:
   	\fIerror\fP:		error value
.br
A status value indicating success or failure is returned; the error value
argument \fIerror\fP passes more detailed information about failures.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_malloc\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		size_t \fIsize\fP,
 		void \fI**ptr\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_malloc\fP allocates \fIsize\fP bytes of memory,
returning the new pointer in \fI*ptr\fP, in the same manner as the
standard C \fImalloc\fP() function, but also optionally tracking
the allocation (see \fBmb_mem_list_enable\fP) and printing debug
messages when \fIverbose\fP is high enough or debugging has been
turned on with \fBmb_mem_debug_on\fP.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_realloc\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		size_t \fIsize\fP,
 		void \fI**ptr\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_realloc\fP resizes the memory block pointed to
by \fI*ptr\fP (allocated by \fBmb_malloc\fP or \fBmb_realloc\fP) to
\fIsize\fP bytes, in the same manner as the standard C
\fIrealloc\fP() function, updating \fI*ptr\fP and any memory
tracking as \fBmb_malloc\fP does.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_free\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI**ptr\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_free\fP frees the memory pointed to by
\fI*ptr\fP (allocated by \fBmb_malloc\fP or \fBmb_realloc\fP), sets
\fI*ptr\fP to NULL, and updates any memory tracking as
\fBmb_malloc\fP does.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_mallocd\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		const char \fI*sourcefile\fP,
 		int \fIsourceline\fP,
 		size_t \fIsize\fP,
 		void \fI**ptr\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_mallocd\fP behaves exactly as \fBmb_malloc\fP
does, but also records the \fIsourcefile\fP name and \fIsourceline\fP
number of the call site in the memory tracking list (when
enabled), to aid in locating memory leaks. \fBMB-System\fP source
code normally invokes this indirectly via the \fBmb_mallocd\fP
macro, which supplies \fB__FILE__\fP and \fB__LINE__\fP
automatically.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_reallocd\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		const char \fI*sourcefile\fP,
 		int \fIsourceline\fP,
 		size_t \fIsize\fP,
 		void \fI**ptr\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_reallocd\fP behaves exactly as \fBmb_realloc\fP
does, but also records the \fIsourcefile\fP name and \fIsourceline\fP
number of the call site, in the same manner as \fBmb_mallocd\fP.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_freed\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		const char \fI*sourcefile\fP,
 		int \fIsourceline\fP,
 		void \fI**ptr\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_freed\fP behaves exactly as \fBmb_free\fP does,
but also records the \fIsourcefile\fP name and \fIsourceline\fP
number of the call site, in the same manner as \fBmb_mallocd\fP.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_mem_list_enable\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_mem_list_enable\fP turns on internal tracking,
by \fBmb_malloc\fP/\fBmb_realloc\fP/\fBmb_free\fP and related
functions, of a list of all currently allocated memory blocks,
enabling the use of \fBmb_memory_status\fP and \fBmb_memory_list\fP.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_mem_list_disable\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_mem_list_disable\fP turns off the memory
allocation tracking enabled by \fBmb_mem_list_enable\fP.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_mem_debug_on\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_mem_debug_on\fP turns on printing of debug
messages by the memory allocation functions above regardless of
the \fIverbose\fP level passed to them.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_mem_debug_off\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_mem_debug_off\fP turns off the unconditional
debug message printing enabled by \fBmb_mem_debug_on\fP.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_memory_clear\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_memory_clear\fP frees every memory block
currently in the allocation list maintained when memory tracking
is enabled (see \fBmb_mem_list_enable\fP), and empties the list.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_memory_status\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		int \fI*nalloc\fP,
 		int \fI*nallocmax\fP,
 		int \fI*overflow\fP,
 		size_t \fI*allocsize\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_memory_status\fP returns, when memory tracking
is enabled (see \fBmb_mem_list_enable\fP), the number of memory
blocks currently allocated (\fI*nalloc\fP), the maximum number of
blocks the tracking list can hold (\fI*nallocmax\fP), a flag
(\fI*overflow\fP) indicating whether that maximum has been
exceeded (in which case tracking becomes incomplete), and the
total number of bytes currently allocated (\fI*allocsize\fP).

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_memory_list\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_memory_list\fP prints to standard error a
listing of every memory block currently in the allocation
tracking list (see \fBmb_mem_list_enable\fP), including the
source file and line number recorded by \fBmb_mallocd\fP.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_update_arrays\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*mbio_ptr\fP,
 		int \fInbath\fP,
 		int \fInamp\fP,
 		int \fInss\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_update_arrays\fP resizes every array registered
with \fBmb_register_array\fP for the \fBMBIO\fP descriptor pointed
to by \fImbio_ptr\fP to match new maximum numbers of bathymetry
beams \fInbath\fP, amplitude beams \fInamp\fP, and sidescan pixels
\fInss\fP. It is called internally as necessary while reading data
whose numbers of beams or pixels can vary from ping to ping.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_update_arrayptr\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*mbio_ptr\fP,
 		void \fI**handle\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_update_arrayptr\fP updates the stored copy of
one registered array pointer \fI*handle\fP for the \fBMBIO\fP
descriptor pointed to by \fImbio_ptr\fP, for use after the calling
program has reallocated that array itself.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_list_arrays\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*mbio_ptr\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_list_arrays\fP prints to standard error a
listing of every array registered with \fBmb_register_array\fP for
the \fBMBIO\fP descriptor pointed to by \fImbio_ptr\fP.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_deall_ioarrays\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*mbio_ptr\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_deall_ioarrays\fP frees every array registered
with \fBmb_register_array\fP for the \fBMBIO\fP descriptor pointed
to by \fImbio_ptr\fP. It is called internally by \fBmb_close\fP.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_get_time\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		int \fItime_i[7]\fP,
 		double \fI*time_d\fP);

The function \fBmb_get_time\fP converts the seven-element time
array \fItime_i\fP (year, month, day, hour, minute, second,
microsecond) into \fI*time_d\fP, the number of seconds since
January 1, 1970 (with no time zone correction applied -
\fBMB-System\fP always works in whatever time standard was used
when the data were logged). Leap days are accounted for; leap
seconds are not.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_get_date\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		double \fItime_d\fP,
 		int \fItime_i[7]\fP);

The function \fBmb_get_date\fP is the inverse of \fBmb_get_time\fP,
converting \fItime_d\fP seconds since January 1, 1970 into the
seven-element time array \fItime_i\fP.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_get_date_string\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		double \fItime_d\fP,
 		char \fI*string\fP);

The function \fBmb_get_date_string\fP converts \fItime_d\fP
seconds since January 1, 1970 into a human-readable date and time
\fIstring\fP.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_get_jtime\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		int \fItime_i[7]\fP,
 		int \fItime_j[5]\fP);

The function \fBmb_get_jtime\fP converts the seven-element time
array \fItime_i\fP into the five-element Julian day time array
\fItime_j\fP (year, Julian day of year, hour, minute, second - with
\fItime_j\fP[4] tracking the residual microseconds), the alternate
time representation used internally by some sonar systems and data
formats.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_get_itime\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		int \fItime_j[5]\fP,
 		int \fItime_i[7]\fP);

The function \fBmb_get_itime\fP is the inverse of
\fBmb_get_jtime\fP, converting the five-element Julian day time
array \fItime_j\fP into the seven-element time array \fItime_i\fP.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
char \fB*mb_day_name\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		int \fIday\fP);

The function \fBmb_day_name\fP returns a pointer to a static
string naming the day of the week numbered 1 (Sunday) through 7
(Saturday) by \fIday\fP; any other value returns "Unknown".

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
char \fB*mb_month_name\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		int \fImonth\fP);

The function \fBmb_month_name\fP returns a pointer to a static
string naming the month numbered 1 (January) through 12 (December)
by \fImonth\fP; any other value returns "Unknown".

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_fix_y2k\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		int \fIyear_short\fP,
 		int \fI*year_long\fP);

The function \fBmb_fix_y2k\fP converts a two digit year value
\fIyear_short\fP into a four digit year \fI*year_long\fP, adding
1900 if \fIyear_short\fP is 62 or greater and 2000 otherwise (no
digital swath sonar data predates 1962, when multibeam sonars were
first patented and built).

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_unfix_y2k\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		int \fIyear_long\fP,
 		int \fI*year_short\fP);

The function \fBmb_unfix_y2k\fP is the inverse of \fBmb_fix_y2k\fP,
converting a four digit year \fIyear_long\fP into a two digit year
\fI*year_short\fP.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_error\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		int \fIerror\fP,
 		char \fI**message\fP);

Given the error value \fIerror\fP, \fBmb_error\fP returns a
short error message in the string **\fImessage\fP. The \fIverbose\fP
value controls the standard error output verbosity of the function.  The return
status value signals success if \fIerror\fP is a recognized \fBMBIO\fP
error value and failure otherwise.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_notice_log_datatype\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*mbio_ptr\fP,
 		int \fIdata_id\fP);

The function \fBmb_notice_log_datatype\fP increments the count, in
the notice list maintained in the \fBMBIO\fP descriptor pointed to
by \fImbio_ptr\fP, of data records of kind \fIdata_id\fP that have
been read or written. This supports the end-of-run notice summary
printed by many \fBMB-System\fP programs.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_notice_log_error\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*mbio_ptr\fP,
 		int \fIerror_id\fP);

The function \fBmb_notice_log_error\fP behaves as
\fBmb_notice_log_datatype\fP does, but increments the count of
occurrences of the (nonfatal) error condition \fIerror_id\fP.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_notice_log_problem\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*mbio_ptr\fP,
 		int \fIproblem_id\fP);

The function \fBmb_notice_log_problem\fP behaves as
\fBmb_notice_log_datatype\fP does, but increments the count of
occurrences of the data problem condition \fIproblem_id\fP (e.g. a
recognized but questionable value encountered while reading).

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_notice_get_list\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*mbio_ptr\fP,
 		int \fI*notice_list\fP);

The function \fBmb_notice_get_list\fP copies the notice list
maintained in the \fBMBIO\fP descriptor pointed to by \fImbio_ptr\fP
(as tallied by \fBmb_notice_log_datatype\fP,
\fBmb_notice_log_error\fP, and \fBmb_notice_log_problem\fP) into
the array \fInotice_list\fP, which must be allocated by the
calling program to hold \fBMB_NOTICE_MAX\fP values.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_notice_message\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		int \fInotice\fP,
 		char \fI**message\fP);

The function \fBmb_notice_message\fP returns, in the string
\fI**message\fP, a short description of the notice list entry
\fInotice\fP (one of the data record, error, or problem
identifiers logged by the \fBmb_notice_log_*\fP functions above).

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_navint_add\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*mbio_ptr\fP,
 		double \fItime_d\fP,
 		double \fIlon_easting\fP,
 		double \fIlat_northing\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_navint_add\fP adds a navigation fix
to a circular buffer of navigation values maintained in
the \fBMBIO\fP data structure pointed to by \fB*mbio_ptr\fP.
This buffer is used to interpolate navigation for data
formats where the navigation is asynchronous (where navigation
and survey pings come in different data records).
The input values are:
 	\fI*mbio_ptr\fP:	pointer to \fBMBIO\fP structure
 	\fItime_d\fP:		time of navigation fix in seconds
 			since 1/1/70 00:00:00
 	\fIlon_easting\fP:	longitude (degrees), or projected
 				easting if the \fBMBIO\fP structure
 				has been set to use a projected
 				coordinate system
 	\fIlat_northing\fP:	latitude (degrees), or projected
 				northing if the \fBMBIO\fP structure
 				has been set to use a projected
 				coordinate system
.br
The return values are:
   	\fIerror\fP:		error value
.br
A status value indicating success or failure is returned; the error value
argument \fIerror\fP passes more detailed information about failures.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_navint_interp\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*mbio_ptr\fP,
 		double \fItime_d\fP,
 		double \fIheading\fP,
 		double \fIrawspeed\fP,
 		double \fI*lon\fP,
 		double \fI*lat\fP,
 		double \fI*speed\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_navint_interp\fP interpolates navigation to
the time \fItime_d\fP using
a circular buffer of navigation values maintained in
the \fBMBIO\fP data structure pointed to by \fB*mbio_ptr\fP.
This buffer is used to interpolate navigation for data
formats where the navigation is asynchronous (where navigation
and survey pings come in different data records).
The input values are:
 	\fI*mbio_ptr\fP:	pointer to \fBMBIO\fP structure
 	\fItime_d\fP:		time of current ping in seconds
 			since 1/1/70 00:00:00
 	\fIheading\fP:		heading in degrees
 	\fIrawspeed\fP:		speed in km/hr
 				(zero if not known)
.br
The return values are:
 	\fI*lon\fP:		longitude (degrees)
 	\fI*lat\fP:		latitude (degrees)
 	\fI*speed\fP:		speed made good in km/hr
   	\fIerror\fP:		error value
.br
A status value indicating success or failure is returned; the error value
argument \fIerror\fP passes more detailed information about failures.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_navint_prjinterp\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*mbio_ptr\fP,
 		double \fItime_d\fP,
 		double \fIheading\fP,
 		double \fIrawspeed\fP,
 		double \fI*easting\fP,
 		double \fI*northing\fP,
 		double \fI*speed\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_navint_prjinterp\fP behaves exactly as
\fBmb_navint_interp\fP does, except that it interpolates and
returns projected easting and northing values (\fI*easting\fP,
\fI*northing\fP) rather than longitude and latitude, for use when
the \fBMBIO\fP structure has been set to work in a projected
coordinate system.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_attint_add\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*mbio_ptr\fP,
 		double \fItime_d\fP,
 		double \fIheave\fP,
 		double \fIroll\fP,
 		double \fIpitch\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_attint_add\fP adds an attitude
(heave, roll, pitch) data point
to a circular buffer of attitude values maintained in
the \fBMBIO\fP data structure pointed to by \fB*mbio_ptr\fP.
This buffer is used to interpolate attitude for data
formats where the attitude is asynchronous (where attitude
and survey pings come in different data records).
The input values are:
 	\fI*mbio_ptr\fP:	pointer to \fBMBIO\fP structure
 	\fItime_d\fP:		time of attitude in seconds
 			since 1/1/70 00:00:00
 	\fIheave\fP:		heave (meters, up +)
 	\fIroll\fP:		roll (degrees, starboard up +)
 	\fIpitch\fP:		pitch (degrees, forward up +)
.br
The return values are:
   	\fIerror\fP:		error value
.br
A status value indicating success or failure is returned; the error value
argument \fIerror\fP passes more detailed information about failures.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_attint_nadd\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*mbio_ptr\fP,
 		int \fInsamples\fP,
 		double \fI*time_d\fP,
 		double \fI*heave\fP,
 		double \fI*roll\fP,
 		double \fI*pitch\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_attint_nadd\fP adds \fInsamples\fP attitude
(heave, roll, pitch) data points at once to the circular buffer
described under \fBmb_attint_add\fP above, which is more efficient
than calling \fBmb_attint_add\fP repeatedly when a block of
attitude samples is available at once (e.g. when merging
externally logged attitude data).

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_attint_interp\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*mbio_ptr\fP,
 		double \fItime_d\fP,
 		double \fI*heave\fP,
 		double \fI*roll\fP,
 		double \fI*pitch\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_attint_interp\fP interpolates attitude
(heave, roll, pitch) data to
the time \fItime_d\fP using
a circular buffer of attitude values maintained in
the \fBMBIO\fP data structure pointed to by \fB*mbio_ptr\fP.
This buffer is used to interpolate attitude for data
formats where the attitude is asynchronous (where attitude
and survey pings come in different data records).
The input values are:
 	\fI*mbio_ptr\fP:	pointer to \fBMBIO\fP structure
 	\fItime_d\fP:		time of current ping in seconds
 			since 1/1/70 00:00:00
.br
The return values are:
 	\fI*heave\fP:		heave (meters, up +)
 	\fI*roll\fP:		roll (degrees, starboard up +)
 	\fI*pitch\fP:		pitch (degrees, forward up +)
   	\fIerror\fP:		error value
.br
A status value indicating success or failure is returned; the error value
argument \fIerror\fP passes more detailed information about failures.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_depint_add\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*mbio_ptr\fP,
 		double \fItime_d\fP,
 		double \fIsensordepth\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_depint_add\fP adds a sensor (transducer) depth
value to a circular buffer of sensor depth values maintained in
the \fBMBIO\fP data structure pointed to by \fB*mbio_ptr\fP, in
the same manner that \fBmb_hedint_add\fP does for heading.

