.br
int \fBmb_apply_time_latency\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		int \fIdata_num\fP,
 		double \fI*data_time_d\fP,
 		int \fItime_latency_mode\fP,
 		double \fItime_latency_static\fP,
 		int \fItime_latency_num\fP,
 		double \fI*time_latency_time_d\fP,
 		double \fI*time_latency_value\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_apply_time_latency\fP corrects, in place, the
\fIdata_num\fP timestamps in \fIdata_time_d\fP for sensor time
latency (clock lag). If \fItime_latency_mode\fP is
\fBMB_SENSOR_TIME_LATENCY_STATIC\fP, a constant
\fItime_latency_static\fP offset (seconds) is subtracted from
every timestamp. If \fItime_latency_mode\fP is
\fBMB_SENSOR_TIME_LATENCY_MODEL\fP, a time-varying latency is
instead interpolated from the \fItime_latency_num\fP-point model
given by \fItime_latency_time_d\fP and \fItime_latency_value\fP
and subtracted from each timestamp.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_apply_time_filter\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		int \fIdata_num\fP,
 		double \fI*data_time_d\fP,
 		double \fI*data_value\fP,
 		double \fIfilter_length\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_apply_time_filter\fP replaces, in place, the
\fIdata_num\fP values in \fIdata_value\fP (timestamped by
\fIdata_time_d\fP) with a running-mean smoothed version computed
over a time window of length \fIfilter_length\fP seconds. This is
used, for example, to smooth noisy externally logged sensor time
series before merging them with swath data.

