\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_get_bounds\fP(
 		char \fI*text\fP,
 		double \fI*bounds\fP);

The function \fBmb_get_bounds\fP parses the string
\fI*text\fP and extracts geographic bounds of a
rectangular region in the form:
 		\fIbounds[0]\fP: minimum longitude
 		\fIbounds[1]\fP: maximum longitude
 		\fIbounds[2]\fP: minimum latitude
 		\fIbounds[3]\fP: maximum latitude
.br
where \fI*text\fP is in the standard \fBGMT\fP bounds form.
The longitude and latitude values in \fI*text\fP
should separated by a '/' character, and individual
values may be represented in decimal degrees or in
"dd:mm:ss" form (dd=degrees, mm=minutes, ss=seconds).

double \fBmb_ddmmss_to_degree\fP(
 		char \fI*text\fP);

The function \fBmb_ddmmss_to_degree\fP parses the string
\fI*text\fP and extracts a decimal longitude or latitude
value from a "dd:mm:ss" (dd=degrees, mm=minutes, ss=seconds)
value.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_apply_lonflip\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		int \fIlonflip\fP,
 		double \fI*longitude\fP);

The function \fBmb_apply_lonflip\fP rewrites \fI*longitude\fP in
place so that it falls in the range required by \fIlonflip\fP: if
\fIlonflip\fP is negative, \fI*longitude\fP is wrapped into
[\-360,0]; if zero, into [\-180,180]; if positive, into [0,360].
See \fBmb_read_init\fP for the general meaning of \fIlonflip\fP.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_coor_scale\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		double \fIlatitude\fP,
 		double \fI*mtodeglon\fP,
 		double \fI*mtodeglat\fP);

The function \fBmb_coor_scale\fP returns the scaling factors
\fI*mtodeglon\fP and \fI*mtodeglat\fP that convert distances in
meters to longitude and latitude differences in degrees at the
given \fIlatitude\fP, using a standard ellipsoidal earth model.
These scale factors are used throughout \fBMB-System\fP to convert
between geographic coordinates and local distances (for example, in
computing sensor lever arm offsets).

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_alvinxy_scale\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		double \fIlatitude\fP,
 		double \fI*mtodeglon\fP,
 		double \fI*mtodeglat\fP);

The function \fBmb_alvinxy_scale\fP returns scaling factors
\fI*mtodeglon\fP and \fI*mtodeglat\fP in the same sense as
\fBmb_coor_scale\fP, but computed using the Clark 1866 spheroid
coefficients used by the WHOI Alvin group's "AlvinXY" local
rectilinear navigation coordinate frame convention (Murphy and
Singh, 2010).

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_proj_init\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		char \fI*projection\fP,
 		void \fI**pjptr\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_proj_init\fP initializes, using the
\fBlibproj\fP cartographic projections library, a map projection
described by \fIprojection\fP (a \fBPROJ\fP/\fBEPSG\fP projection
specification string, as also accepted by the \fB\-J\fP options of
many \fBMB-System\fP and \fBGMT\fP programs) and returns a pointer
to the initialized projection in \fI*pjptr\fP for use by
\fBmb_proj_forward\fP and \fBmb_proj_inverse\fP.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_proj_free\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI**pjptr\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_proj_free\fP deallocates a projection
previously initialized by \fBmb_proj_init\fP and pointed to by
\fI*pjptr\fP.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_proj_forward\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*pjptr\fP,
 		double \fIlon\fP,
 		double \fIlat\fP,
 		double \fI*easting\fP,
 		double \fI*northing\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_proj_forward\fP applies the forward map
projection \fIpjptr\fP (initialized by \fBmb_proj_init\fP) to
convert geographic longitude \fIlon\fP and latitude \fIlat\fP
(degrees) into projected \fI*easting\fP and \fI*northing\fP
(meters).

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_proj_inverse\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*pjptr\fP,
 		double \fIeasting\fP,
 		double \fInorthing\fP,
 		double \fI*lon\fP,
 		double \fI*lat\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_proj_inverse\fP applies the inverse of the map
projection \fIpjptr\fP (initialized by \fBmb_proj_init\fP) to
convert projected \fIeasting\fP and \fInorthing\fP (meters) back
into geographic \fI*lon\fP and \fI*lat\fP (degrees).

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_spline_init\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		const double \fI*x\fP,
 		const double \fI*y\fP,
 		int \fIn\fP,
 		double \fIyp1\fP,
 		double \fIypn\fP,
 		double \fI*y2\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_spline_init\fP computes the second-derivative
array \fI*y2\fP of a natural cubic spline through the \fIn\fP data
points (\fIx\fP[i], \fIy\fP[i]), given the first-derivative
boundary conditions \fIyp1\fP and \fIypn\fP at the two endpoints
(following Press et al., \fINumerical Recipes in C\fP). The array
\fI*y2\fP is then passed to \fBmb_spline_interp\fP to evaluate the
spline at arbitrary points.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_spline_interp\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		const double \fI*xa\fP,
 		const double \fI*ya\fP,
 		double \fI*y2a\fP,
 		int \fIn\fP,
 		double \fIx\fP,
 		double \fI*y\fP,
 		int \fI*i\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_spline_interp\fP evaluates, at the point
\fIx\fP, the cubic spline defined by the \fIn\fP data points
(\fIxa\fP[i], \fIya\fP[i]) and the second-derivative array
\fIy2a\fP computed by \fBmb_spline_init\fP, returning the
interpolated value in \fI*y\fP. The index \fI*i\fP should be
initialized to zero before the first call and is thereafter
maintained across successive calls to speed up the table lookup
when \fIx\fP values are supplied in increasing order.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_linear_interp\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		const double \fI*xa\fP,
 		const double \fI*ya\fP,
 		int \fIn\fP,
 		double \fIx\fP,
 		double \fI*y\fP,
 		int \fI*i\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_linear_interp\fP evaluates, at the point
\fIx\fP, simple linear interpolation (or, outside the data range,
extrapolation using the nearest endpoint) through the \fIn\fP data
points (\fIxa\fP[i], \fIya\fP[i]), returning the interpolated value
in \fI*y\fP. The index \fI*i\fP is used in the same way as in
\fBmb_spline_interp\fP.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_linear_interp_longitude\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		const double \fI*xa\fP,
 		const double \fI*ya\fP,
 		int \fIn\fP,
 		double \fIx\fP,
 		double \fI*y\fP,
 		int \fI*i\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_linear_interp_longitude\fP behaves as
\fBmb_linear_interp\fP does, but treats the \fIya\fP values as
longitudes, so that interpolation is done correctly across the
+/\-180 degree dateline discontinuity.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_linear_interp_latitude\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		const double \fI*xa\fP,
 		const double \fI*ya\fP,
 		int \fIn\fP,
 		double \fIx\fP,
 		double \fI*y\fP,
 		int \fI*i\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_linear_interp_latitude\fP behaves as
\fBmb_linear_interp\fP does, but treats the \fIya\fP values as
latitudes, without extrapolating beyond +/\-90 degrees.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_linear_interp_heading\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		const double \fI*xa\fP,
 		const double \fI*ya\fP,
 		int \fIn\fP,
 		double \fIx\fP,
 		double \fI*y\fP,
 		int \fI*i\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_linear_interp_heading\fP behaves as
\fBmb_linear_interp\fP does, but treats the \fIya\fP values as
headings, so that interpolation is done correctly across the
0/360 degree discontinuity.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_takeoff_to_rollpitch\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		double \fItheta\fP,
 		double \fIphi\fP,
 		double \fI*alpha\fP,
 		double \fI*beta\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_takeoff_to_rollpitch\fP
translates angles from the "takeoff" coordinate
reference frame to the "rollpitch" coordinate system.
See the discussion of coordinate systems below.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_rollpitch_to_takeoff\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		double \fIalpha\fP,
 		double \fIbeta\fP,
 		double \fI*theta\fP,
 		double \fI*phi\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_rollpitch_to_takeoff\fP
translates angles from the "rollpitch" coordinate
reference frame to the "takeoff" coordinate system.
See the discussion of coordinate systems below.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_xyz_to_takeoff\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		double \fIx\fP,
 		double \fIy\fP,
 		double \fIz\fP,
 		double \fI*theta\fP,
 		double \fI*phi\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_xyz_to_takeoff\fP converts a Cartesian beam
direction vector (\fIx\fP, \fIy\fP, \fIz\fP) into the "takeoff"
coordinate system angles \fI*theta\fP (from vertical) and
\fI*phi\fP (from the alongtrack direction). See the discussion of
coordinate systems below.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_lever\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		double \fIsonar_offset_x\fP,
 		double \fIsonar_offset_y\fP,
 		double \fIsonar_offset_z\fP,
 		double \fInav_offset_x\fP,
 		double \fInav_offset_y\fP,
 		double \fInav_offset_z\fP,
 		double \fIvru_offset_x\fP,
 		double \fIvru_offset_y\fP,
 		double \fIvru_offset_z\fP,
 		double \fIvru_pitch\fP,
 		double \fIvru_roll\fP,
 		double \fI*lever_x\fP,
 		double \fI*lever_y\fP,
 		double \fI*lever_z\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_lever\fP computes the effective lever arm
correction (\fI*lever_x\fP, \fI*lever_y\fP, \fI*lever_z\fP) that
must be applied to sonar position because of the relative offsets
between the sonar, the navigation antenna, and the attitude
(VRU) sensor, given the current \fIvru_pitch\fP and \fIvru_roll\fP.
This older, simpler lever arm calculation has largely been
superseded by the fuller sensor platform model implemented by
\fBmb_platform_lever\fP below, but remains available for formats
and programs that use it directly.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_beaudoin\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		mb_3D_orientation \fItx_align\fP,
 		mb_3D_orientation \fItx_orientation\fP,
 		double \fItx_steer\fP,
 		mb_3D_orientation \fIrx_align\fP,
 		mb_3D_orientation \fIrx_orientation\fP,
 		double \fIrx_steer\fP,
 		double \fIreference_heading\fP,
 		double \fI*beamAzimuth\fP,
 		double \fI*beamDepression\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_beaudoin\fP implements the algorithm of
Beaudoin et al. for computing the true beam azimuth
\fI*beamAzimuth\fP and depression angle \fI*beamDepression\fP of a
multibeam sonar beam given the transmit and receive array
mounting alignments (\fItx_align\fP, \fIrx_align\fP), the vessel
orientations at ping and receive time (\fItx_orientation\fP,
\fIrx_orientation\fP), the transmit and receive beam steering
angles (\fItx_steer\fP, \fIrx_steer\fP), and a \fIreference_heading\fP.
This properly accounts for the different vessel attitudes at
transmit and receive time on a moving platform.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_beaudoin_unrotate\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		mb_3D_vector \fIorig\fP,
 		mb_3D_orientation \fIrotate\fP,
 		mb_3D_vector \fI*final\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_beaudoin_unrotate\fP rotates the vector
\fIorig\fP by the inverse of the roll, pitch, and heading
orientation \fIrotate\fP, returning the result in \fI*final\fP.
This is a supporting calculation used by \fBmb_beaudoin\fP.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_platform_init\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI**platform_ptr\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_platform_init\fP allocates and initializes an
empty sensor platform model, returning a pointer to it in
\fI*platform_ptr\fP. A platform model records the survey platform
(ship, ROV, or AUV) identity together with a list of sensors, each
with its position and attitude offsets relative to the platform's
reference point, and is used throughout \fBMBIO\fP (see
\fBmb_set_platform\fP, \fBmb_extract_platform\fP, and
\fBmb_preprocess\fP) to convert raw sensor readings and geometry
into properly located and oriented bathymetry, in place of the
older, simpler \fBmb_lever\fP calculation. See also the
\fBmbmakeplatform\fP manual page and the platform file format
description therein.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_platform_setinfo\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*platform_ptr\fP,
 		int \fItype\fP,
 		char \fI*name\fP,
 		char \fI*organization\fP,
 		char \fI*documentation_url\fP,
 		double \fIstart_time_d\fP,
 		double \fIend_time_d\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_platform_setinfo\fP sets the identifying
information of the platform pointed to by \fIplatform_ptr\fP: its
\fItype\fP (e.g. surface ship, ROV, AUV), \fIname\fP,
\fIorganization\fP, a \fIdocumentation_url\fP, and the time
interval (\fIstart_time_d\fP to \fIend_time_d\fP) over which this
platform definition applies.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_platform_add_sensor\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*platform_ptr\fP,
 		int \fItype\fP,
 		mb_longname \fImodel\fP,
 		mb_longname \fImanufacturer\fP,
 		mb_longname \fIserialnumber\fP,
 		int \fIcapability1\fP,
 		int \fIcapability2\fP,
 		int \fInum_offsets\fP,
 		int \fInum_time_latency\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_platform_add_sensor\fP appends a new sensor
record to the platform pointed to by \fIplatform_ptr\fP, of the
given \fItype\fP (e.g. sonar, INS, GPS), identified by \fImodel\fP,
\fImanufacturer\fP, and \fIserialnumber\fP, with capability flags
\fIcapability1\fP and \fIcapability2\fP describing what the sensor
measures, and space allocated for \fInum_offsets\fP position/attitude
offset records (see \fBmb_platform_set_sensor_offset\fP) and
\fInum_time_latency\fP time latency model samples (see
\fBmb_platform_set_sensor_timelatency\fP). Sensors are indexed in
the order added, starting from zero.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_platform_set_sensor_offset\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*platform_ptr\fP,
 		int \fIisensor\fP,
 		int \fIioffset\fP,
 		double \fIposition_offset_x\fP,
 		double \fIposition_offset_y\fP,
 		double \fIposition_offset_z\fP,
 		double \fIattitude_offset_azimuth\fP,
 		double \fIattitude_offset_roll\fP,
 		double \fIattitude_offset_pitch\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_platform_set_sensor_offset\fP sets the
\fIioffset\fPth position offset (\fIposition_offset_x\fP,
\fIposition_offset_y\fP, \fIposition_offset_z\fP, in the platform's
forward/starboard/down reference frame, meters) and attitude
offset (\fIattitude_offset_azimuth\fP, \fIattitude_offset_roll\fP,
\fIattitude_offset_pitch\fP, degrees) of sensor number \fIisensor\fP
of the platform pointed to by \fIplatform_ptr\fP, relative to the
platform reference point.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_platform_set_sensor_timelatency\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*platform_ptr\fP,
 		int \fIisensor\fP,
 		int \fItime_latency_mode\fP,
 		double \fItime_latency_static\fP,
 		int \fInum_time_latency\fP,
 		double \fI*time_latency_time_d\fP,
 		double \fI*time_latency_value\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_platform_set_sensor_timelatency\fP sets the
time latency model for sensor number \fIisensor\fP of the platform
pointed to by \fIplatform_ptr\fP, in the same sense as
\fBmb_apply_time_latency\fP: if \fItime_latency_mode\fP is
\fBMB_SENSOR_TIME_LATENCY_STATIC\fP a constant
\fItime_latency_static\fP offset is used, and if
\fBMB_SENSOR_TIME_LATENCY_MODEL\fP the \fInum_time_latency\fP-point
model given by \fItime_latency_time_d\fP and \fItime_latency_value\fP
is used instead.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_platform_set_sensor_flipsign_heading\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*platform_ptr\fP,
 		int \fIisensor\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);
.br
int \fBmb_platform_set_sensor_flipsign_roll\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*platform_ptr\fP,
 		int \fIisensor\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);
.br
int \fBmb_platform_set_sensor_flipsign_pitch\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*platform_ptr\fP,
 		int \fIisensor\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The functions \fBmb_platform_set_sensor_flipsign_heading\fP,
\fBmb_platform_set_sensor_flipsign_roll\fP, and
\fBmb_platform_set_sensor_flipsign_pitch\fP each mark sensor number
\fIisensor\fP of the platform pointed to by \fIplatform_ptr\fP as
having its heading, roll, or pitch sign convention reversed
relative to the \fBMB-System\fP standard, so that
\fBmb_platform_apply_flipsign_heading\fP and
\fBmb_platform_apply_flipsign_attitude\fP will correct it.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_platform_set_source_sensor\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*platform_ptr\fP,
 		int \fIsource_type\fP,
 		int \fIsensor\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_platform_set_source_sensor\fP designates sensor
number \fIsensor\fP of the platform pointed to by \fIplatform_ptr\fP
as the source of a particular kind of data (\fIsource_type\fP -
e.g. navigation, heading, roll/pitch, heave, or sonar depth), so
that functions such as \fBmb_platform_orientation\fP know which
sensor's offsets to apply.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_platform_deall\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI**platform_ptr\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_platform_deall\fP deallocates a platform model
previously allocated by \fBmb_platform_init\fP or
\fBmb_platform_read\fP and pointed to by \fI*platform_ptr\fP.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_platform_read\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		char \fI*platform_file\fP,
 		void \fI**platform_ptr\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_platform_read\fP reads a platform definition
from the file \fIplatform_file\fP (as written by
\fBmb_platform_write\fP or the \fBmbmakeplatform\fP program) and
returns a newly allocated platform model in \fI*platform_ptr\fP.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_platform_write\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		char \fI*platform_file\fP,
 		void \fI*platform_ptr\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_platform_write\fP writes the platform model
pointed to by \fIplatform_ptr\fP to the file \fIplatform_file\fP,
in the format read by \fBmb_platform_read\fP.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_platform_lever\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*platform_ptr\fP,
 		int \fItargetsensor\fP,
 		int \fItargetsensoroffset\fP,
 		double \fIheading\fP,
 		double \fIroll\fP,
 		double \fIpitch\fP,
 		double \fI*lever_x\fP,
 		double \fI*lever_y\fP,
 		double \fI*lever_z\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_platform_lever\fP computes the lever arm
correction (\fI*lever_x\fP, \fI*lever_y\fP, \fI*lever_z\fP, in the
platform reference frame) between the platform's navigation
reference point and offset number \fItargetsensoroffset\fP of
sensor number \fItargetsensor\fP of the platform pointed to by
\fIplatform_ptr\fP, given the platform's current \fIheading\fP,
\fIroll\fP, and \fIpitch\fP. This generalizes the older
\fBmb_lever\fP calculation to the full multi-sensor platform model.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_platform_position\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*platform_ptr\fP,
 		int \fItargetsensor\fP,
 		int \fItargetsensoroffset\fP,
 		double \fInavlon\fP,
 		double \fInavlat\fP,
 		double \fIsensordepth\fP,
 		double \fIheading\fP,
 		double \fIroll\fP,
 		double \fIpitch\fP,
 		double \fI*targetlon\fP,
 		double \fI*targetlat\fP,
 		double \fI*targetz\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_platform_position\fP translates a navigation fix
(\fInavlon\fP, \fInavlat\fP, \fIsensordepth\fP) obtained at the
platform's navigation reference point, together with the current
\fIheading\fP, \fIroll\fP, and \fIpitch\fP, into the corresponding
position (\fI*targetlon\fP, \fI*targetlat\fP, \fI*targetz\fP) of
offset number \fItargetsensoroffset\fP of sensor number
\fItargetsensor\fP of the platform pointed to by \fIplatform_ptr\fP,
applying the lever arm computed by \fBmb_platform_lever\fP.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_platform_position_offset\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*platform_ptr\fP,
 		int \fItargetsensor\fP,
 		int \fItargetsensoroffset\fP,
 		double \fI*target_x_offset\fP,
 		double \fI*target_y_offset\fP,
 		double \fI*target_z_offset\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_platform_position_offset\fP returns just the
position offset (\fI*target_x_offset\fP, \fI*target_y_offset\fP,
\fI*target_z_offset\fP, in the platform reference frame) of offset
number \fItargetsensoroffset\fP of sensor number \fItargetsensor\fP
of the platform pointed to by \fIplatform_ptr\fP, without applying
any attitude rotation.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_platform_apply_flipsign_attitude\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*platform_ptr\fP,
 		double \fI*roll\fP,
 		double \fI*pitch\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_platform_apply_flipsign_attitude\fP negates
\fI*roll\fP and/or \fI*pitch\fP in place if the corresponding
attitude source sensor of the platform pointed to by
\fIplatform_ptr\fP has been marked with
\fBmb_platform_set_sensor_flipsign_roll\fP or
\fBmb_platform_set_sensor_flipsign_pitch\fP.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_platform_apply_flipsign_heading\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*platform_ptr\fP,
 		double \fI*heading\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_platform_apply_flipsign_heading\fP corrects
\fI*heading\fP in place if the heading source sensor of the
platform pointed to by \fIplatform_ptr\fP has been marked with
\fBmb_platform_set_sensor_flipsign_heading\fP.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_platform_orientation\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*platform_ptr\fP,
 		double \fIheading\fP,
 		double \fIroll\fP,
 		double \fIpitch\fP,
 		double \fI*platform_heading\fP,
 		double \fI*platform_roll\fP,
 		double \fI*platform_pitch\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_platform_orientation\fP converts a raw
\fIheading\fP, \fIroll\fP, and \fIpitch\fP reading from the
platform's designated attitude and heading source sensors (see
\fBmb_platform_set_source_sensor\fP) into the equivalent platform
reference frame orientation (\fI*platform_heading\fP,
\fI*platform_roll\fP, \fI*platform_pitch\fP), correcting for that
sensor's mounting angle offsets.

\---------------------------------------------------------
.br
int \fBmb_platform_orientation_offset\fP(
 		int \fIverbose\fP,
 		void \fI*platform_ptr\fP,
 		int \fItargetsensor\fP,
 		int \fItargetsensoroffset\fP,
 		double \fI*target_hdg_offset\fP,
 		double \fI*target_roll_offset\fP,
 		double \fI*target_pitch_offset\fP,
 		int \fI*error\fP);

The function \fBmb_platform_orientation_offset\fP returns just the
